  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Thursday)\nIssued at 221 PM CDT FRI AUG 14 2026\n\nThis afternoon, surface high pressure is present across the\nNorthern Gulf. Today is a prime example that ridging doesn't\nalways equal rain-free as we are very hot, humid, and unstable\nacross Central Alabama. In the upper levels, a ridge is centered\njust to the north across the Southeast US with weak flow across\nCentral Alabama. At the surface, little change is expected\nthrough the short term (today through Saturday). However, aloft,\nthe ridge is expected to strengthen and expand westward. This\nshould help to suppress overall convection more toward isolated,\nand definitely reinforce the heat over us. In the near term, any\nstorms that do pop up this afternoon and evening could grow with\ngusty winds and brief heavy rainfall with our unstable airmass.\n\nOutside of any storms, the heat will be the main story. We\ncontinue with a heat advisory for all of Central Alabama for\ntoday through Sunday. There will not be any major features to\nhelp to cool the area off, so look for a high probability of heat\nadvisories to be continued through the beginning of next week.\nThe majority of areas should see max heat indices of 105 to 109.\nWhile there could be an isolated 110 plus at times, coverage of\nhigher indices should be isolated. There is a medium to high\nchance that some areas (especially west) could see triple digit\nhigh temperatures by Saturday into the beginning of next week.\n\n08",
